<Docs
  path="/docs/jarvis"
  heading="JARVIS"
  lead="JARVIS is the intelligence inside KalCode. Give it an objective and it coordinates providers, threads and missions on your behalf — within your permissions."
>
  <JarvisLockup sizes="(min-width: 56rem) 18rem, min(calc(100vw - 5rem), 18rem)" />

  <h2>What JARVIS does</h2>
  <p>
    JARVIS is a conversational command interface over the same runtime you see on the Dashboard.
    You can talk to it about your projects and hand it objectives. It is designed to:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>plan an objective and break it into threads,</li>
    <li>delegate each thread to the provider that fits — Claude Code, Codex or Gemini CLI,</li>
    <li>follow progress through structured runtime events,</li>
    <li>and report back with the evidence: what ran, what changed, and what was verified.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>JARVIS is part of KalCode, not a separate product. You can use KalCode without it.</p>

  <h2>Missions and verification</h2>
  <p>
    Larger objectives become missions: outcome-oriented, multi-step work. A mission is not marked
    done because a model says it is. It needs verification evidence, such as tests that ran, checks
    that passed, or a diff you have reviewed.
  </p>

  <h2>JARVIS is not above your authority</h2>
  <p>
    Every action JARVIS takes, and every action taken by a thread it started, goes through the same
    permission model as any other agent. JARVIS cannot raise its own permissions, cannot approve its
    own requests, and can never enable Bypass. When something needs your decision, it waits for you.
  </p>

  <h2>What JARVIS sees</h2>
  <p>
    JARVIS works from KalCode's local runtime: your workspaces, threads, events and memory, which stay
    on your device by default. Memory is scoped and inspectable, so you can see what it holds.
  </p>
</Docs>
